  <h2>Fix the leak first</h2>
  <p>Close the fixture's valve or the main shutoff, and fix or replace the failed part before you start drying. Drying around a slow drip is drying forever. See <a href="/guides/appliance-leaks/">appliance and toilet leaks</a> for the valves and the hoses that fail most.</p>

  <h2>The five checks</h2>

  <h3>1. What kind of water was it?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clean</strong> (a supply line, the sink's cold or hot line, rain through a window): DIY is possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Gray</strong> (dishwasher or washing machine water, a toilet overflow without feces): be more careful; porous materials it soaked often need to go.</li>
    <li><strong>Black</strong> (sewage, toilet with feces, floodwater): <strong>call a professional.</strong> Don't clean it yourself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>More detail in <a href="/guides/water-damage-categories/">clean, gray or black water</a>.</p>

  <h3>2. How long was it wet?</h3>
  <p>The EPA advises drying wet materials within <strong>24 to 48 hours</strong> to prevent mold. A leak you caught within hours is a very different job from one you found under the sink after a week. <strong>Wet for more than 48 hours, or an unknown time? Lean toward a professional assessment.</strong></p>

  <h3>3. What got wet?</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Usually</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tile, sealed concrete, metal, plastic</td><td>Dries well; wipe and ventilate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Plywood</td><td>Often dries well with airflow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Particleboard or MDF (cabinet bases, some subfloors)</td><td>Swells and crumbles; often needs replacing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Drywall</td><td>Wicks water upward; can dry if caught quickly, otherwise the bottom section is cut out</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Carpet and pad</td><td>Pad usually needs replacing after soaking; carpet can sometimes be saved if clean water and quick</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hardwood flooring</td><td>Can cup; needs slow, controlled drying, often professional</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Insulation</td><td>Wet insulation in walls or ceilings usually has to come out</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>4. How big is it?</h3>
  <p>The EPA's mold guidance uses about <strong>10 square feet</strong> (roughly 3 feet by 3 feet) as the line below which a homeowner can usually handle cleanup. The same rough size works as a gut check for water: a wet patch you could cover with a couple of bath towels is a DIY candidate; a wet room isn't.</p>

  <h3>5. Has it spread where you can't see?</h3>
  <p>This is what turns "small" into "big". Look and feel for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Flooring that's <strong>soft, cupped or lifting</strong> at the edges.</li>
    <li><strong>Swelling</strong> at the bottom of cabinets or baseboards.</li>
    <li><strong>Stains on the ceiling below</strong> if the leak was upstairs.</li>
    <li>A <strong>musty smell</strong> that appears a day or two later.</li>
    <li>Readings from a <strong>moisture meter</strong> at the bottom of the nearest walls and in the floor. An inexpensive pin meter from a hardware store is worth having in any house.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>More signs in <a href="/guides/signs-of-hidden-water-damage/">how to find hidden water damage</a>.</p>

  <h2>The decision</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>DIY is reasonable if…</th><th>Call a professional if…</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Clean water</td><td>Gray or black water</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Caught within hours</td><td>Wet more than 48 hours, or unknown</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hard surfaces you can see and reach</td><td>Under flooring, inside walls, in insulation or the ceiling below</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small patch</td><td>A room or more</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dry within a day or two, confirmed with a meter</td><td>Still damp after 48 hours, or a musty smell</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No one in the house with asthma or a weakened immune system</td><td>Vulnerable people at home</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How to dry it properly yourself</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Remove standing water</strong> with towels or a wet/dry vacuum (never a household vacuum).</li>
    <li><strong>Empty and open up</strong> the space: take everything out of the cabinet, remove the toe-kick if you can, pull back a loose baseboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Take out what can't be saved:</strong> soaked cardboard, swollen particleboard shelves, sodden pad.</li>
    <li><strong>Move air and remove moisture:</strong> a fan pointed into the space and a dehumidifier in the room, doors closed. Open windows only if the air outside is dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Check it twice a day</strong> by touch and with a moisture meter, comparing with a dry spot elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Close up only when it's really dry,</strong> not when it looks dry.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If it isn't dry within about 48 hours, stop and get a professional assessment. That's how long drying should take with professional equipment; see <a href="/guides/how-long-does-water-damage-take-to-dry/">typical drying times</a>.</p>

  <h2>If it's bigger than it looked</h2>
  <p>Make it safe, photograph everything and call for help the same day; read <a href="/guides/call-insurance-or-restoration-company-first/">insurance or restoration company first?</a> Local restoration help is listed in each state section:
    {STATE_SUMMARIES.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={`/${s.slug}/`}>{s.name}</a></>)}.
  </p>
